    {
      "id": "notes",
      "kind": "edit.markdownNote",
      "title": "How to Play",
      "executionState": "enabled",
      "parameters": {
        "text": "# Bee nectar quest\nRun once to open the meadow. The Trackpad shows the actual image. Click or drag onto a highlighted flower, then release: one turn runs and a flower hit adds **one nectar point**. A click on empty space keeps the score. The opening frame never scores. Repeat clicks can collect more nectar.\n\nThe JSON State inspector shows `score`, `turn`, and `lastFlower`. Stop Loop finishes the current turn. Run continues the committed state; Reset State starts over. Load State restores a `.state.json` checkpoint and Run recreates the scene. Every completed PNG has state and turn-journal sidecars.\n\nSDXS renders the complete scene each turn; flower placement can change. SAM masks describe the displayed frame. Missing flowers cannot score. The initial JSON state defines the location, visual style, lighting, bee appearance and flower entities. A flower hit writes `bee.action` from the actual mask label. The prompt starts with the state-owned bee appearance and action; Qwen supplies compact scene context and composition. The render seed advances each turn. There is no fixed flower placement. Swift graph nodes own scoring and actions.\n\nTo change the scene, edit the JSON State node while stopped, then Reset State and Run. Keep `subjects` and `entityIDsByLabel` consistent with the entity labels. The `label:` entry represents no flower; every other mapping key is `label:` followed by its exact SAM query. Rebuild the app and Reset State after reloading this version if your current session came from the old workflow. The inspector lists detected subjects; only detected flowers can score.\n\nRelink the SDXS, Qwen3 no-thinking and SAM 3.1 Core AI packages if requested."
      },
      "resources": {}
